{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjWc2oFYKasMByLSv3Lo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Apv6Ka7TCF269KwuvLiyr6","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"Start of line, before the first barline: two beamed eighths, then a half note with a stroke through its stem. The first eighth sits low, below the staff on ledger lines (C2). The second is an octave higher (C3). The stroked half note is the copyist's shorthand for repeated eighths, here C3 C3 C3 C3. That is bar 42: (C2 C3) C3 C3 C3 C3. It is not bar 41, which is six G2 eighths. Nothing on this line looks like bar 41.\nSecond stretch: six beamed eighths that go low, up, back, down, back, up. That is B2 C3 B2 A2 B2 C3, bar 43.\nThird stretch: the same figure, bar 44.\nFourth stretch: the same figure again, bar 45.\nLast stretch: two beamed eighths (B2 C3), a lower quarter (A2), then a quarter rest before the final barline. That is bar 46, and the line ends with it.\nSo the line holds bars 42 to 46. The end matches the program. The start does not.","right":false,"insights":"Bars 43 to 45 repeat the same figure, so the line's position depends on its edge bars. The opening bar is hard to read: the copyist wrote four repeated C3 eighths as one stroked half note, and the low C2 sits on ledger lines far below the staff. Recognising that shorthand, and checking that the octave jump C2 to C3 fits bar 42 and not the all-G bar 41, settles the start."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5308,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5308},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"03694355-7781-4d